+This matters on
+.Fx
+because the two common browsers behave differently on a machine with no
+usable GPU, such as a virtual machine or a headless server.
+.Bl -tag -width "www/linux-chrome"
+.It www/firefox
+Works as installed.
+Firefox keeps its software WebGL fallback enabled by default, so no special
+invocation is needed.
+.It www/linux-chrome
+Needs an explicit flag:
+.Bd -literal -offset indent
+google-chrome --enable-unsafe-swiftshader \e
+    http://localhost:8080/
+.Ed
 .Pp
+This is the Linux binary running under
+.Xr linux 4
+emulation, so it has no access to the host GPU, and recent Chrome releases
+have deprecated the automatic fallback to software WebGL.
+Started without the flag it has no WebGL context at all, and the interface
+hangs as described above.
+The flag selects a software implementation, which is the intended use here:
+the page is served locally and is trusted.
+.El
+.Pp
+The same flag applies to the native
+.Pa www/chromium
+port, and to other Chromium-derived browsers, when they cannot reach a GPU.
+.Pp
